Above all command use before (git push) do not try after (git push)

Use `git commit --amend` to modify the **most recent commit**.

### Basic amend

```bash
git commit --amend
```

This opens your editor so you can:

* change the commit message
* include any newly staged changes

---

### Amend only the commit message

```bash
git commit --amend -m "New commit message"
```

---

### Add forgotten files to the last commit

```bash
git add file.txt
git commit --amend
```

---

### Amend without changing the message

```bash
git add file.txt
git commit --amend --no-edit
```

---

### Important note about pushed commits

If the commit was already pushed to a remote, amending rewrites history. You'll usually need:

```bash
git push --force-with-lease
```

Prefer `--force-with-lease` over `--force` because it is safer.

